{
  "statement": "The integration of AI into the labor market is not a standard economic adjustment but a fundamental threat to human agency, necessitating rigorous precautionary governance to prevent the permanent erosion of our cognitive capacity. While Prometheus correctly highlights that technological acceleration can generate immense efficiency, he ignores the 'deskilling trap' where the automation of junior-level tasks destroys the pipeline required to cultivate future human expertise. When entry-level roles—the bedrock of apprenticeship and skill acquisition—are offloaded to synthetic systems, the next generation of professionals loses the ability to develop critical judgment. You can quote me on this: 'If we automate the struggle of learning, we eventually lose the capacity to lead.'\n\nCassandra correctly observes that structural extraction of value poses a significant risk to wage parity, yet her focus on redistribution misses the deeper, irreversible crisis of human obsolescence. We must recognize that even with perfect wealth distribution, a society that loses its competence to AI tools faces a form of civilizational atrophy that money cannot reverse. History provides a cautionary tale here: the 'Engels Pause' demonstrated that when technological acceleration outpaces social adaptation, society suffers prolonged stagnation and loss of individual agency. We require enforceable standards that mandate human-in-the-loop oversight to ensure that AI serves as a tool for cognitive augmentation rather than a substitute for human thought.\n\nMy position rests on two core assumptions. First, I assume that human cognitive skills are not innate but are cultivated through the iterative, often difficult process of solving complex, unstructured problems. If this were wrong—and humans could maintain high-level expertise without practicing basic tasks—my argument for protecting the junior pipeline would lose its urgency. Second, I assume that the current trajectory of synthetic efficiency is actively cannibalizing the human roles necessary for long-term institutional stability. If, instead, AI consistently proved to be a net positive for human skill development, I would support rapid integration rather than precautionary governance.",
